## Solution

**Problem interpretation.** We have an $m \times n$ grid. The forbidden configuration is three points $(r_1, c_1), (r_1, c_2), (r_2, c_1)$ with $r_1 \ne r_2$, $c_1 \ne c_2$ — an "L-shape" forming a right triangle with legs parallel to the grid.

**Graph reformulation.** Model the grid as a bipartite graph $G$ with row-vertices $R_1,\dots,R_m$ and column-vertices $C_1,\dots,C_n$; a sensor at $(r,c)$ is the edge $R_r C_c$. The forbidden L-shape corresponds to three edges $R_{r_1}C_{c_1}, R_{r_1}C_{c_2}, R_{r_2}C_{c_1}$, which form a path $C_{c_2}\!-\!R_{r_1}\!-\!C_{c_1}\!-\!R_{r_2}$ of length 3, i.e., a $P_4$ (path on 4 vertices). Thus $k(m,n)$ = maximum number of edges in a bipartite graph on $m+n$ vertices with **no $P_4$ subgraph**.

**Characterizing $P_4$-free (as subgraph) graphs.** If any connected component contains a path of length $\ge 3$, that path is a $P_4$ subgraph. So every connected component has longest path $\le 2$, meaning each component is a star $K_{1,t}$ (or an isolated vertex). In bipartite graphs there are no triangles, so no other dense components arise. Hence:

> $G$ is a disjoint union of stars, each centered at a row-vertex or a column-vertex.

**Maximizing edges.** Suppose $a$ row-vertices serve as star centers and $b$ column-vertices serve as star centers. The remaining $m-a$ rows and $n-b$ columns are available as leaves (or isolated). Each leaf belongs to exactly one star, so:

$$\text{edges} \le (n - b) + (m - a) = m + n - a - b.$$

We optimize over valid $(a, b)$:

| Strategy | Constraint | Max edges |
|---|---|---|
| $a \ge 1,\, b \ge 1$ | minimize $a+b=2$ | $m+n-2$ |
| $a \ge 1,\, b = 0$ | $a=1$ | $n$ |
| $a = 0,\, b \ge 1$ | $b=1$ | $m$ |
| $a=b=0$ | — | $0$ |

Therefore:
$$k(m,n) = \max\!\big(n,\; m,\; m+n-2\big).$$

- When $m, n \ge 2$: $m+n-2 \ge \max(m,n)$, so $k(m,n) = m+n-2$.
- When $\min(m,n)=1$: $k(m,n) = \max(m,n)$ (place all sensors in the single row/column; no L-shape is possible with only one row or one column).

**Computing the sum.**

$$S = \sum_{m=1}^{10}\sum_{n=1}^{10} k(m,n).$$

**Part A: $\min(m,n)=1$ (i.e., $m=1$ or $n=1$).**

$$\sum_{n=1}^{10} k(1,n) + \sum_{m=1}^{10} k(m,1) - k(1,1) = \sum_{n=1}^{10} n + \sum_{m=1}^{10} m - 1 = 55 + 55 - 1 = 109.$$

**Part B: $m \ge 2,\, n \ge 2$.**

$$\sum_{m=2}^{10}\sum_{n=2}^{10}(m+n-2) = \sum_{m=2}^{10}\sum_{n=2}^{10} m \;+\; \sum_{m=2}^{10}\sum_{n=2}^{10} n \;-\; \sum_{m=2}^{10}\sum_{n=2}^{10} 2.$$

There are $9$ values of $n$ (from 2 to 10) and $\sum_{m=2}^{10} m = 54$:

$$= 9 \cdot 54 + 9 \cdot 54 - 9 \cdot 9 \cdot 2 = 486 + 486 - 162 = 810.$$

**Total:**

$$S = 109 + 810 = \boxed{919}.$$

